WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.267
Interviews.

2
00:00:01.547 --> 00:00:05.515
Day 84 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Career phase.

3
00:00:07.265 --> 00:00:08.289
The key idea.

4
00:00:08.569 --> 00:00:13.945
An interview is a structured conversation between two people deciding whether to work together.

5
00:00:14.225 --> 00:00:15.569
You're interviewing them too.

6
00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:18.303
Prepare stories, not memorised answers.

7
00:00:19.653 --> 00:00:20.506
Part 1.

8
00:00:20.786 --> 00:00:23.453
Tell me about yourself (60 to 90 seconds).

9
00:00:24.703 --> 00:00:26.687
Present, then Past, then Future.

10
00:00:28.037 --> 00:00:28.975
You could say:

11
00:00:29.255 --> 00:00:38.173
I'm a backend engineer with 15 years of experience, currently leading a team of 6 building a grocery marketplace on Java and Kafka (present).

12
00:00:38.453 --> 00:00:45.749
Before that, I spent 8 years in fintech, where I scaled a payments platform to 2 million transactions a day (past).

13
00:00:46.029 --> 00:00:53.453
I'm looking for a role where I can shape architecture and grow engineers at a larger scale, and that's what drew me to this team (future).

14
00:00:54.803 --> 00:00:55.891
Beginner version.

15
00:00:56.171 --> 00:00:57.109
You could say:

16
00:00:57.389 --> 00:01:06.093
I recently graduated in Computer Science, and I interned at XYZ, where I built a REST API for inventory tracking in Spring Boot.

17
00:01:06.373 --> 00:01:14.800
I enjoy backend work and learning how systems scale, and I'm excited about this role because your team works with Kafka and microservices.

18
00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:17.025
Part 2.

19
00:01:17.305 --> 00:01:18.713
Behavioural questions:

20
00:01:18.993 --> 00:01:19.782
STAR.

21
00:01:21.032 --> 00:01:23.507
Prepare 6 to 8 stories that you can adapt:

22
00:01:24.857 --> 00:01:26.201
Here is a quick table:

23
00:01:26.481 --> 00:01:28.273
Theme, and Example question.

24
00:01:28.553 --> 00:01:29.406
Conflict:

25
00:01:29.686 --> 00:01:31.905
Tell me about a disagreement with a teammate.

26
00:01:32.185 --> 00:01:33.038
Failure:

27
00:01:33.318 --> 00:01:34.918
Tell me about a time you failed.

28
00:01:35.198 --> 00:01:36.115
Leadership:

29
00:01:36.395 --> 00:01:38.187
When did you lead without authority?

30
00:01:38.467 --> 00:01:39.278
Pressure:

31
00:01:39.558 --> 00:01:41.691
Describe a tight deadline you handled.

32
00:01:41.971 --> 00:01:42.761
Impact:

33
00:01:43.041 --> 00:01:44.875
What's the project you're proudest of?

34
00:01:45.155 --> 00:01:45.945
Learning:

35
00:01:46.225 --> 00:01:47.931
When did you learn something quickly?

36
00:01:48.211 --> 00:01:49.086
Feedback:

37
00:01:49.366 --> 00:01:51.713
Tell me about critical feedback you received.

38
00:01:51.993 --> 00:01:52.867
Decision:

39
00:01:53.147 --> 00:01:55.259
Describe a hard technical trade-off.

40
00:01:56.609 --> 00:01:57.484
Example:

41
00:01:57.764 --> 00:01:59.236
STAR answer (failure).

42
00:01:59.516 --> 00:02:00.199
S:

43
00:02:00.479 --> 00:02:05.705
Our release went out with a data-migration bug that corrupted 2% of user addresses.

44
00:02:05.985 --> 00:02:06.689
T:

45
00:02:06.969 --> 00:02:09.657
As tech lead, I owned the fix and the follow-up.

46
00:02:09.937 --> 00:02:10.556
A:

47
00:02:10.836 --> 00:02:19.071
I rolled back within 20 minutes, wrote a repair script, personally emailed affected users, and added migration dry-runs to CI.

48
00:02:19.351 --> 00:02:20.076
R:

49
00:02:20.356 --> 00:02:24.047
All data restored in 4 hours; no repeat in 2 years.

50
00:02:24.327 --> 00:02:29.233
What I learned: never run migrations without a dry-run on a production snapshot.

51
00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:31.479
Part 3.

52
00:02:31.759 --> 00:02:33.637
Questions to ask the interviewer.

53
00:02:34.887 --> 00:02:37.617
What does success look like in the first 90 days?

54
00:02:37.897 --> 00:02:40.628
What's the biggest challenge the team is facing right now?

55
00:02:40.908 --> 00:02:42.956
How are technical decisions made here?

56
00:02:43.236 --> 00:02:44.879
How do engineers grow here?

57
00:02:45.159 --> 00:02:47.036
What did the last promotion look like?

58
00:02:47.316 --> 00:02:49.940
What do you personally enjoy most about working here?

59
00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:52.207
Part 4.

60
00:02:52.487 --> 00:02:53.938
Handling tricky moments.

61
00:02:55.188 --> 00:02:56.532
Here is a quick table:

62
00:02:56.812 --> 00:02:57.943
Moment, and Say.

63
00:02:58.223 --> 00:02:59.545
You don't know the answer:

64
00:02:59.825 --> 00:03:01.660
I haven't worked with that directly.

65
00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:03.433
Here's how I'd approach it...

66
00:03:03.713 --> 00:03:05.036
You need time to think:

67
00:03:05.316 --> 00:03:07.279
Let me take a moment to think about that.

68
00:03:07.559 --> 00:03:09.095
Salary question (early):

69
00:03:09.375 --> 00:03:11.487
I'd like to learn more about the role first.

70
00:03:11.767 --> 00:03:13.281
What's the budgeted range?

71
00:03:13.561 --> 00:03:14.713
Why are you leaving?:

72
00:03:14.993 --> 00:03:16.124
Stay positive:

73
00:03:16.404 --> 00:03:18.495
I'm looking for bigger scale and ownership.

74
00:03:18.775 --> 00:03:20.631
Never criticise your current employer.

75
00:03:20.911 --> 00:03:21.999
Any weaknesses?:

76
00:03:22.279 --> 00:03:24.519
A real one + what you're doing about it:

77
00:03:24.799 --> 00:03:27.103
I used to over-explain to executives.

78
00:03:27.383 --> 00:03:29.495
I now practise one-sentence summaries.

79
00:03:30.845 --> 00:03:31.805
Part 5.

80
00:03:32.085 --> 00:03:33.471
Practice conversation:

81
00:03:33.751 --> 00:03:35.522
The 'Tell me about yourself' moment.

82
00:03:36.772 --> 00:03:38.799
Here is how the conversation goes.

83
00:03:39.079 --> 00:03:40.145
Let's start.

84
00:03:40.425 --> 00:03:42.324
Could you tell me a little about yourself?

85
00:03:42.604 --> 00:03:43.329
Sure.

86
00:03:43.609 --> 00:03:47.151
I'm a backend engineer with nine years of experience.

87
00:03:47.431 --> 00:03:53.447
Right now I lead a team of five building a grocery delivery platform on Java and Kafka.

88
00:03:53.727 --> 00:03:55.263
What did you do before that?

89
00:03:55.543 --> 00:04:02.113
I spent six years in fintech, where I helped scale a payments system to two million transactions a day.

90
00:04:03.463 --> 00:04:05.063
The conversation continues.

91
00:04:05.343 --> 00:04:07.306
And why are you interested in this role?

92
00:04:07.586 --> 00:04:12.706
I'd like to work on bigger distributed systems and grow into an architect role.

93
00:04:12.986 --> 00:04:15.418
Your platform team does exactly that.

94
00:04:15.698 --> 00:04:16.637
Great.

95
00:04:16.917 --> 00:04:20.031
Tell me about a hard technical decision you made recently.

96
00:04:21.381 --> 00:04:22.917
That's the end of this lesson.

97
00:04:23.197 --> 00:04:27.848
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

98
00:04:28.128 --> 00:04:34.251
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

